G. ''brücke'', a bridge, = Icel. ''bryggja'' = Sw. ''brygga'' = Dan. ''brygge'', a pier, landing-stage, gangway, rarely a bridge; connected with Icel. ''brū'' = Sw. ''bro'' = Dan. ''bro'', a bridge, a paved way. Perhaps akin to ''brow''; cf. OBulg. ''brŭvĭ'', a bridge, also brow: see ''brow''.] '''1.''' Any structure which spans a body of water, or a valley, road, or the like, and affords passage or conveyance. {{smaller|Bridges are made of various materials, principally stone, iron, and wood, and in a great variety of forms. In an ''arch-'' or ''arched bridge'' the passage or roadway is carried by an arch or arches, which are supported by abutments or by piers.
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Panel-truss Bridge.}}}}
Such bridges are constructed of brick, stone, iron, steel, or wood. Brick is seldom used alone, except for comparatively small spans, and for unimportant work when stone cannot readily be obtained. In more important works it is often combined with stone, which is introduced to bind, to distribute pressure, to protect the more exposed portions, and for architectural effect. Stone, wherever it can be used, is the most valuable material, on account of its massiveness, stability of form, and resistance to the elements; but it is inferior to iron in
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Common Truss Bridge.}}}}
economy, facility of construction, and ready adaptability to various situations. Among the finest monuments of antiquity are ranked the remains of Roman arched stone bridges. The largest stone}}

